'''Agriculture in [[Balboa]]''' comprises the cultivation of crops, livestock production, and aquaculture across the country's five provinces. Once the dominant sector of the Balboan economy, agriculture now accounts for approximately 9 per cent of GDP — down from over 40 per cent during the [[Republic of Balboa (1899–1973)|rump state]] period — and employs approximately 7 per cent of the labour force. The sector is defined by the structural overvaluation of the [[International Dollar]] (ID), which makes Balboan agricultural exports expensive on world markets and cheap imports competitive domestically, and by the cooperative farm system established under the [[Twelve Guarantees]] land reform programme, which shapes the sector's ownership structure, labour relations, and political economy.

Only one crop — highland [[Coffee industry of Balboa|coffee]] — is genuinely competitive on the international market. The remaining agricultural exports — sugar, bananas, tropical fruits, and cacao — are kept viable by the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]] and government price supports, which offset the overvalued ID. Even within the [[BTPMEU]], Balboan agricultural products cannot compete with Tapuyan production on price. The purpose of this support is not to build competitive export industries but to sustain employment: the cooperative farms employ a large share of the rural population, and their collapse would trigger mass rural-to-urban migration and undermine the post-reunification settlement.

== Land use ==

Balboa has a total land area of 296,086 square kilometres. The country's geography — a narrow isthmus divided by the [[Yalabirga Mountain Range]], with the Aethiopian (windward) coast receiving heavy rainfall and the Shendan (leeward) coast lying in the rain shadow — creates sharply contrasting agricultural zones.

