The principal agricultural zones are:

* '''Jala highlands''' (Aethiopian-facing slopes of the Yalabirga, 800–1,500m): The country's sole coffee-growing region, with volcanic soils and orographic rainfall (2,000–4,000mm/year). Also produces cacao, tropical fruits, and cotton. Dominated by cooperative farms.
* '''Migjorn lowlands''' (Aethiopian coastal plain, 0–300m): Sugar cane, bananas, and rice on the hot, humid coastal plain (1,500–2,500mm/year). Mixed cooperative and commercial operations.
* '''Nurra coastal plain and savanna''' (Aethiopian coast, 0–400m): Palm oil plantations and cattle ranches on the coastal plain and interior savanna. Primarily commercial operations operated by [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]].
* '''Tramuntana highlands and savanna''' (Shendan side, 400–1,200m): Cattle ranching on the savanna and subsistence agriculture in the highland valleys. The rain shadow climate (800–1,200mm/year on the coastal plain) limits crop production. The poorest and least productive agricultural region.
* '''Estret corridor''' (canal zone, 0–100m): Minimal agricultural activity; the province is dominated by the canal, logistics, and services. Less than 5 per cent of provincial GDP comes from agriculture.

