=== Coffee ===

{{main|Coffee industry of Balboa}}

Highland arabica coffee is Balboa's only genuinely competitive agricultural export. Approximately 56,324 tonnes per year are produced exclusively in the [[Jala Province|Jala]] highlands, on the Aethiopian-facing slopes of the Yalabirga range. Balboan coffee is positioned as a premium specialty origin, comparable to {{w|Jamaican Blue Mountain|Jamaican Blue Mountain}} or {{w|Kona coffee|Kona}}, and is exported to [[Illypnia]]n and [[Okaiken]] markets. The principal producers are [[Grup Llibertat]] and [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]], with processing by [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]]'s [[Cafè Tramuntana]] division. A growing number of independent specialty roasters in [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]] source directly from Jala cooperatives.

=== Sugar ===

Sugar cane is grown on the Migjorn lowlands and the Aethiopian coastal plain, where the hot, humid climate and reliable rainfall provide ideal growing conditions. Annual production is approximately 4.5 million tonnes of cane, yielding approximately 500,000 tonnes of refined sugar. The sector is dominated by [[Grup Llibertat]] (cooperative plantations in Migjorn) and [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]] (commercial plantations in Migjorn and Nurra). Sugar refining is concentrated at [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]]'s Refineria de Sucre de Jala in Sant Bart and a second refinery in Migjorn. Refined sugar is exported worldwide, though it is viable only because of the [[Règim de compensació a l'exportació|negative tariff]], which allows the cooperative farms to sell their surplus at prices that cover production costs. Sugar receives the largest single agricultural disbursement from the negative tariff (~$340 million/year), reflecting the large workforce dependent on the sector.

=== Bananas ===

Bananas are grown on the Migjorn lowlands and the Aethiopian coastal plain. Annual production is approximately 900,000 tonnes, making bananas the largest agricultural export by volume. The sector is operated by [[Grup Llibertat]] (cooperative plantations) and [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]] (commercial plantations), with packaging and export handled by [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]]'s Empaquetadora de Bananes de l'Estret division. Bananas are exported through the [[Balboa Canal]] and the [[Port de Portnou]] to world markets, viable only because of the negative tariff. The negative tariff disbursement for bananas is approximately $260 million/year, the second-largest agricultural rebate.

=== Tropical fruits ===

Tropical fruits are grown across the Aethiopian coastal lowlands and the Jala highland valleys. Annual production is approximately 600,000 tonnes, comprising:

* '''Mangoes''' (180,000 tonnes): The largest tropical fruit crop by volume, grown primarily on the Aethiopian coastal plain. Mangoes are the most significant tropical fruit export, with approximately 120,000 tonnes exported annually.
* '''Pineapples''' (150,000 tonnes): Grown on the Aethiopian coastal lowlands, with approximately 100,000 tonnes exported. Pineapples are a major input for Hirocorp's canned fruit and juice operations.
* '''Citrus''' (100,000 tonnes): Oranges and limes grown in the Jala highland valleys and the coastal lowlands. Primarily for domestic consumption, with approximately 20,000 tonnes exported.
* '''Avocados''' (50,000 tonnes): Grown in the Jala highland valleys, where the cooler climate is suitable. Avocados command premium prices and approximately 30,000 tonnes are exported.
* '''Papayas''' (80,000 tonnes): Grown on the Aethiopian coastal plain, with approximately 40,000 tonnes exported.
* '''Other tropical fruits''' (40,000 tonnes): Plantains, guava, passion fruit, and other minor fruits grown in the coastal lowlands and highland valleys. Approximately 15,000 tonnes are exported.

The sector is dominated by cooperative farms, with processing (canning, juicing) by [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]] and export by [[Mercaderies del Canal]]. Tropical fruits receive approximately $200 million/year in negative tariff rebates. As with sugar and bananas, tropical fruit exports are viable only because of the negative tariff; Tapuyan and Sinchew producers can grow the same crops more cheaply.

=== Cacao ===

Cacao is grown in the Jala highlands and the Migjorn lowlands. Annual production is approximately 45,000 tonnes of cacao beans. The principal producer is [[Grup Llibertat]], with processing by [[Hirocorp, S.A.|Hirocorp]]'s Cacau de Migjorn division, which produces cocoa powder, cocoa butter, and chocolate products under the [[Xocolata Migjorn]] brand. Cacao and cocoa products receive approximately $140 million/year in negative tariff rebates.

=== Cotton ===

Cotton is grown in the Jala highlands and on the Tramuntana savanna. Annual production is approximately 65,000 tonnes. The sector is operated by [[Grup Llibertat]] (Jala) and [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]] (Tramuntana). Cotton is primarily sold to the domestic textile industry, with smaller volumes exported to BTPMEU markets.

=== Palm oil ===

Palm oil is produced on the Nurra coastal plain, where [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]] operates plantations established in the 1980s. Annual production is approximately 350,000 tonnes of crude palm oil. The palm oil sector has been the subject of environmental controversy, with criticism from environmental organisations over deforestation in the Nurra coastal plain and from [[Birú]] land-rights groups over the inclusion of traditional Birú territories in CAI's lowland properties. CAI operates a palm oil refinery in Nurra.

=== Rice ===

Rice is grown in the Migjorn lowlands, where the hot, humid climate and irrigated paddies support production. Annual production is approximately 200,000 tonnes, which covers approximately 60 per cent of domestic consumption. The remaining 40 per cent is imported from [[Tapuya]] and [[Sinchew]] through the BTPMEU free trade framework. Rice is produced by cooperative farms in Migjorn and is primarily for domestic consumption rather than export.

== Livestock and aquaculture ==

=== Beef cattle ===

Beef cattle are raised on the Tramuntana savanna and the Nurra coastal plain. The sector is dominated by [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme|CAI]], which operates cattle ranches in both provinces. Annual beef production is approximately 95,000 tonnes. The Tramuntana savanna, with its drier rain-shadow climate, is better suited to grazing than to crop production, and cattle ranching is the primary agricultural activity in the province. Beef is almost entirely for domestic consumption; the overvalued ID makes Balboan beef uncompetitive as an export, and the sector is not covered by significant negative tariff disbursements.

=== Aquaculture and fisheries ===

Aquaculture and marine fisheries are concentrated along the Tramuntana coast (Shendan side) and the Aethiopian coast. Annual production is approximately 120,000 tonnes, comprising:

* '''Shrimp''' (40,000 tonnes): Farmed along the Tramuntana and Migjorn coasts, where mangrove nurseries provide ideal growing conditions. Shrimp is the highest-value aquaculture product, commanding approximately $7,000/tonne. Approximately 20,000 tonnes are exported — certain shrimp species are endemic to Balboa's coastal waters and cannot be sourced from Tapuya or Potocsí, making them inherently competitive regardless of the overvalued ID.
* '''Marine fish''' (50,000 tonnes): Caught by coastal fishing fleets along both the Aethiopian and Shendan coasts, comprising species such as snapper, grouper, and mackerel. Approximately 5,000 tonnes are exported; the remainder is consumed domestically. Overfishing in the coastal zones has been identified as an environmental concern.
* '''Tilapia''' (30,000 tonnes): Farmed in freshwater ponds in the Migjorn lowlands and the Jala highland valleys. Tilapia is a low-cost protein source primarily for domestic consumption, with approximately 5,000 tonnes exported.

The mangrove forests of the Estret and Migjorn coasts serve as critical nursery habitat for commercially important fish and crustacean species, and the expansion of aquaculture — particularly shrimp farming — has created tensions with mangrove conservation efforts.

== Farming systems ==

=== Cooperative sector ===

The cooperative farm system is the dominant structure of Balboan agriculture, covering approximately 60 per cent of agricultural land. The cooperatives were established under the [[Balboan Land and Reparations Authority]] (BLRA) land reform programme, which expropriated white-owned plantations with compensation and reorganised them as worker cooperatives, with former plantation workers granted shares. The system is most extensive in the Jala highlands (coffee, cacao, tropical fruits) and the Migjorn lowlands (sugar, bananas, rice).

The cooperative model is defined by democratic governance — each worker-member has one vote regardless of shareholding — and by the distribution of surplus in proportion to labour contribution. The cooperatives are politically significant as the product of the [[Twelve Guarantees]] land reform, and their continued support is a cornerstone of the [[Balboan People's Party]] (BPP) rural platform.

The cooperative sector has been criticised for lower productivity than the former plantations. Consensus-based governance has slowed investment in mechanisation, irrigation, and diversification, and the fragmentation of land into cooperative holdings has prevented the economies of scale achieved by commercial operators. Agricultural productivity in the cooperative sector has stagnated since the 1990s. The [[Democratic Alliance (Balboa)|Democratic Alliance]] (DA) has advocated for reforms allowing private investment and land consolidation, while the [[New Socialist Party (Balboa)|New Socialist Party]] (PSN) defends the cooperative model and opposes privatisation.

=== Commercial sector ===

The commercial farming sector covers approximately 30 per cent of agricultural land and is dominated by [[Corporació Agrícola de l'Istme]] (CAI), the largest listed agricultural operator. CAI operates the lowland sugar and banana plantations, the Nurra palm oil estates, and the Tramuntana cattle ranches — operations that were considered too large-scale and capital-intensive for the cooperative model. The commercial sector employs wage labour rather than worker-membership and uses precision agriculture techniques, mechanised equipment, and professional management.

The distinction between the cooperative and commercial sectors reflects the ideological divide at the heart of the land reform: the BPP's left wing argues that the lowland operations should have been organised as cooperatives, while the centrist wing maintains that the capital requirements of large-scale lowland agriculture made the corporate model necessary.

=== Subsistence farming ===

Subsistence agriculture persists in the Tramuntana highland valleys, where [[Birú]] communities and poor white Balboan farmers practice small-scale cultivation of maize, cassava, root vegetables, and beans for household consumption. Tramuntana is the poorest province in Balboa, with limited infrastructure, low levels of educational attainment, and high rates of out-migration to [[Sant Cristòfor]] and [[Sant Bart]]. Subsistence farms are not integrated into the cooperative system or the commercial supply chain and contribute minimally to agricultural GDP. The government provides limited extension services and infrastructure investment to these communities, though the remoteness of the highland valleys and the small scale of production make meaningful intervention difficult.

